1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,089
Penny has never paid a cent for her favourite apps.

2
00:00:03,410 --> 00:00:12,050
Not one. And yet last year, the company behind Facebook, Instagram and WhatsApp made two hundred and one billion dollars.

3
00:00:12,369 --> 00:00:14,759
So if Penny isn't paying, who is?

4
00:00:15,410 --> 00:00:19,230
Last episode, the casino let Penny play for free drinks.

5
00:00:19,550 --> 00:00:23,050
Free apps run a quieter version of the same deal.

6
00:00:23,370 --> 00:00:29,449
Here are the three engines that turn a free app into one of the biggest money machines on Earth.

7
00:00:30,100 --> 00:00:31,829
Engine number one:

8
00:00:32,149 --> 00:00:37,310
attention. Every time Penny scrolls, a few of the posts aren't posts.

9
00:00:37,630 --> 00:00:42,070
They're ads. And advertisers pay for every one she sees.

10
00:00:42,390 --> 00:00:47,719
In twenty twenty-five, ninety-eight percent of Meta's money came from advertising.

11
00:00:48,039 --> 00:00:51,179
One hundred and ninety-six billion dollars.

12
00:00:51,829 --> 00:00:54,070
So the business has two levers.

13
00:00:54,390 --> 00:00:57,200
Show more ads, or charge more for each one.

14
00:00:57,520 --> 00:00:59,700
Last year, Meta pulled both.

15
00:01:00,020 --> 00:01:05,400
Ad impressions were up twelve percent, and the average price per ad was up nine.

16
00:01:05,719 --> 00:01:08,659
Which means the real product isn't the app.

17
00:01:08,980 --> 00:01:11,650
It's the hours Penny spends inside it.

18
00:01:11,969 --> 00:01:15,870
Every extra minute she scrolls is more inventory to sell.

19
00:01:16,519 --> 00:01:18,439
And Meta isn't alone.

20
00:01:18,760 --> 00:01:23,299
Google's parent company runs the same play with search and YouTube.

21
00:01:23,620 --> 00:01:29,379
In just the last three months of twenty twenty-five, it made eighty-two billion dollars from ads.

22
00:01:29,700 --> 00:01:33,439
More than eleven billion of that came from YouTube alone.

23
00:01:33,760 --> 00:01:37,319
Free to watch. Very much not free to run.

24
00:01:37,969 --> 00:01:39,719
Engine number two:

25
00:01:40,040 --> 00:01:47,189
data. An ad for running shoes is worth more if it's shown to someone who just searched for running shoes.

26
00:01:47,510 --> 00:01:55,310
So every like, every search, every pause on a video helps the app guess what Penny wants and when she's most likely to buy it.

27
00:01:55,959 --> 00:01:59,609
That's why targeted ads sell for more than random ones.

28
00:01:59,930 --> 00:02:01,700
Penny isn't the customer.

29
00:02:02,019 --> 00:02:04,519
The advertisers are the customers.

30
00:02:04,840 --> 00:02:09,750
Penny's attention, and everything the app learns about her, is what's being sold.

31
00:02:10,400 --> 00:02:12,169
Engine number three:

32
00:02:12,490 --> 00:02:16,219
the auction. The price of an ad isn't fixed.

33
00:02:16,539 --> 00:02:25,800
Every time a slot opens on Penny's screen, advertisers compete for it in an automatic auction that runs in a fraction of a second.

34
00:02:26,120 --> 00:02:30,750
The more advertisers want people like Penny, the higher the price goes.

35
00:02:31,400 --> 00:02:33,909
And it runs on a staggering scale.

36
00:02:34,229 --> 00:02:39,550
In December, three point five eight billion people used Meta's apps every day.

37
00:02:39,870 --> 00:02:46,349
Divide the ad money by all those people, and each one is worth roughly fifty-five dollars a year.

38
00:02:46,669 --> 00:02:48,610
Penny never sees a bill.

39
00:02:48,930 --> 00:02:51,110
But somebody is paying for her.

40
00:02:51,759 --> 00:02:55,750
Here's the clearest proof of what Penny's attention is worth.

41
00:02:56,069 --> 00:03:03,340
When European regulators pushed back on tracking, Meta started offering people in Europe a version with no ads.

42
00:03:03,659 --> 00:03:08,159
The price? Starting at nine ninety-nine euros a month.

43
00:03:08,479 --> 00:03:12,620
That's roughly what the company thinks your attention is worth to it.

44
00:03:13,270 --> 00:03:14,979
So here's the machine.

45
00:03:15,300 --> 00:03:17,650
Attention creates the ad space.

46
00:03:17,969 --> 00:03:19,840
Data makes it valuable.

47
00:03:20,159 --> 00:03:22,919
And the auction squeezes out the highest price.

48
00:03:23,240 --> 00:03:25,310
The app doesn't need your money.

49
00:03:25,629 --> 00:03:27,139
It needs your time.

50
00:03:27,460 --> 00:03:29,530
So spend it on purpose.

51
00:03:29,849 --> 00:03:34,090
One: turn off notifications you didn't ask for.

52
00:03:34,409 --> 00:03:37,250
Every ping is an invitation back in.

53
00:03:37,569 --> 00:03:42,439
Two: check your ad settings, and limit the tracking you're comfortable with.

54
00:03:42,759 --> 00:03:48,030
Three: set a daily screen-time limit, because your hours are the product.

55
00:03:48,680 --> 00:03:50,639
Free apps sell your time.

56
00:03:50,960 --> 00:03:54,460
But some apps want your money too, every month, forever.

57
00:03:54,780 --> 00:03:57,830
The subscription trap, next on Money Mechanics.

